Feature: Shell tests backdate file mtimes portably on both BSD and GNU userlands

  The suite under swarmforge/scripts/test/ is the ONLY quality gate for the
  Babashka/Clojure layer (engineering.prompt, Startup Tools). Six of its tests
  backdate a fixture's mtime using GNU-only relative-time syntax
  (`touch -d "2 hours ago"`, `date -d "90 seconds ago"`), which stock BSD
  userland rejects outright. macOS is a declared target OS and is the host the
  swarm actually runs on, so those six tests are red there today.

  The fix is one shared portable helper plus a standing guard, mirroring the
  existing `portable_stat_lib.sh` (BSD/GNU split behind one function) and
  BL-872's standing-guard placement.

  # BL-874 portable-relative-mtime-01
  Scenario Outline: the helper backdates a file by a relative interval on either userland
    Given a file whose mtime is the current time
    And the host provides only the <userland> date and touch implementations
    When the helper is asked to backdate that file by "<interval>"
    Then the call succeeds
    And the file's mtime is <seconds> seconds earlier than it was, within a 2 second tolerance

    Examples:
      | userland | interval      | seconds |
      | BSD      | 2 hours ago   | 7200    |
      | BSD      | 90 seconds ago| 90      |
      | GNU      | 2 hours ago   | 7200    |
      | GNU      | 90 seconds ago| 90      |

  # BL-874 portable-relative-mtime-02
  Scenario Outline: each previously red test passes on a BSD userland
    Given the test script "<test>"
    When it is run on a host with only BSD date and touch on PATH
    Then it exits zero
    And its output contains no "illegal time specification" or "illegal option" error

    Examples:
      | test                                                       |
      | test_operator_runtime_sandbox_sweep.sh                     |
      | test_operator_runtime_sandbox_sweep_bounded_progress.sh    |
      | test_operator_runtime_fixture_reaper_sweep.sh              |
      | test_operator_runtime_fixture_reaper_sweep_bounded_progress.sh |
      | test_operator_runtime_tick.sh                              |
      | test_handoffd_stuck_escalation_email_wiring.sh             |

  # BL-874 portable-relative-mtime-03
  Scenario: the remediated tree has zero GNU-only relative-time invocations
    Given the tree under swarmforge/scripts after remediation
    When the portable-time guard scans it
    Then it reports zero violations

  # BL-874 portable-relative-mtime-04
  Scenario Outline: the guard flags a newly introduced GNU-only relative-time call
    Given a file under swarmforge/scripts containing <invocation>
    When the portable-time guard scans it
    Then it reports that file as a violation
    And it names the offending line

    Examples:
      | invocation                  |
      | touch -d "2 hours ago" f    |
      | touch -d "-5 minutes" f     |
      | date -d "90 seconds ago"    |
      | date --date="yesterday"     |

  # BL-874 portable-relative-mtime-05
  Scenario Outline: the guard does not flag portable absolute forms
    Given a file under swarmforge/scripts containing <invocation>
    When the portable-time guard scans it
    Then it reports zero violations

    Examples:
      | invocation                       |
      | touch -t 202601010000 f          |
      | touch -r reference_file f        |
      | touch -d "2026-08-10T10:00:00" f |

  # BL-874 portable-relative-mtime-06
  Scenario: the guard runs in the suite every parcel already runs
    Given a file under swarmforge/scripts is edited to introduce a GNU-only relative-time call
    When the extension unit suite is run
    Then the suite fails
    And it names the introducing file
